course: medicine
lesson: 57
title: Gaming nights and the morning after
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A football game on a console trains your thumbs; a real match needs legs, lungs, eyes and a rested brain, and a late gaming night takes from exactly those.
explain: Kai plays EA FC till 2 am, «One more, bro, it's giving Ballon d'Or» («it's giving» is young slang for it feels like), and wakes at 8 with 6 hours instead of 10: a great night for his thumbs, a rough one for everything else.
explain: Games are built to make you want the next match, time feels shorter while you play, and anger at a last-minute loss pumps out adrenaline, so the brain stays switched on long after the console is off.
explain: Lily's mum in Perth has a rule, «Arvo is for gaming, night is for sleeping, no whinging» (arvo is Australian for afternoon, whinging is moaning), and Lily sets her own timer, so the stop is calm and sleep comes quicker.
fact: A console game trains thumbs and quick eyes, but not running, balance, touch on a real ball or fitness.
fact: Each game of a football video game takes about 15 minutes, so «just one more» eight times is about 2 hours.
fact: Anger and excitement release adrenaline, which speeds up the heart and wakes the brain, and it takes time to calm down before sleep.
fact: A tired brain is worse at controlling temper, so players short of sleep argue more and get booked more easily.
fact: In football, two yellow cards in one match make a red card, and the player is sent off.
fact: After a night with no food the body needs fuel; skipping breakfast can leave a player dizzy or wobbly.
fact: Phone buzzes and lights can pull you out of deep sleep even if you don't remember waking, and broken sleep refreshes you less.
fact: A whole night awake, like at a sleepover where nobody sleeps, makes balance, reactions and judgement much worse.
fact: Energy drinks are full of caffeine and often sugar; they hide tiredness for a while, then you feel worse, and most big UK supermarkets stopped selling them to under-16s in 2018.
fact: Stopping screens at a fixed time, with a quiet wind-down before bed, helps children fall asleep faster.
fact: A very tired brain can switch off for a second or two, called a microsleep, even with the eyes open.
fact: «Mardy» is Northern English for grumpy and sulky, «wee buns» is Northern Irish for dead easy, and «yaar» is Hindi and Urdu for mate.
exercise: Kai games till 2 am and gets up at 8. How much sleep is that? | 6 hours | 8 hours | 10 hours | 4 hours
exercise: What does a night of console football train? | Mostly the thumbs and eyes | The legs and lungs, like a real match | The whole body equally, head to toe | Real ball skills for the next game
exercise: Ryan says «just one more game» eight times, about 15 minutes each. How long? | About 2 hours | About 15 minutes | About 30 minutes | About 8 hours
exercise: Mason loses in the last minute at 11 pm and lies there buzzing. Why can't he sleep? | Anger released adrenaline | Losing makes you sleepy | The controller was too heavy | Games always relax you
exercise: Ruby stayed up gaming and gets two yellow cards. What links them? | Tired brains lose their temper faster | Gaming makes you play fairer | Refs pick on tired players | Yellow cards come from bad boots
exercise: Two yellow cards in one match add up to what? | A red card, and you're sent off | A free kick for your team from the halfway line | A penalty and a goal for the other team | Nothing, the second one cancels the first
exercise: Noah wakes at 8:40 for a 9:00 kick-off and skips breakfast. Likely second half? | Low on energy and wobbly | Lighter and much faster than usual | Exactly as good as ever | Full of energy all game
exercise: Ciaran keeps his phone buzzing by his pillow all night. What happens to his sleep? | It is broken and refreshes him less | It is untouched if he doesn't wake fully | It gets deeper with every buzz | It makes him need less sleep
exercise: Dev plays FIFA 15 hours a week and trains 2. How many hours really move his legs? | 2 hours | 17 hours | 15 hours | 13 hours
exercise: Harvey pulls an all-nighter, then plays a cup game. What suffers most? | Balance, reactions and judgement | Only his voice | Nothing, kids don't need sleep | His thumbs only
exercise: Tyler wants an energy drink to wake up after gaming. What's the real fix? | Sleep, not a can of caffeine | A bigger can of energy drink | Two cans instead of one | A can before and at half-time
exercise: Why does Lily's game timer help her sleep? | A fixed stop gives a calm wind-down | Timers make every game shorter and easier | It makes her play faster | Timers cut the electricity off
exercise: Maya stares into space mid-match after a late night. What might it be? | A microsleep of a second or two | A sign she is very fit and resting her legs | A trick to fool the defenders into relaxing | A normal part of hockey that everyone does
exercise: Which is a sensible gaming rule for a school night? | Console off well before bedtime | Play till you finally win | Play in bed with the lights off | No rule, sleep comes anyway
exercise: Ruby is «properly mardy» at the match. What is she? | Grumpy and sulky | Happy and chatty | Fast and strong | Very hungry
exercise: Ciaran says it'll be «wee buns». What does he mean? | It'll be dead easy | He wants a cake after the match | He's very tired and wants his bed | It'll be a draw, nil all
test: Dev from Mumbai: «Yaar, 17 hours of football a week, I'm basically pro». What's wrong with his sum? | Only the 2 hours on a pitch train his body | Gaming hours count double | He's under-counting his football | Nothing, it's all football
test: Kai's cousin: «Gaming relaxes you, you'll be asleep in seconds». After an exciting game? | It winds you up, not down | You fall asleep instantly | Your heart slows right down | You sleep extra deeply
test: What would happen if Harvey's sleepover agreed lights-out at 10 pm before his 10 am game? | He'd play far better than after no sleep | He'd play exactly the same | He'd be more tired than with none | He'd have to miss the game
test: Mason screams «ref you're blind!» at a computer ref. Next day, short on sleep, what's he likelier to do in a real match? | Argue with the real ref | Stay perfectly calm all game long | Score a hat-trick in the first half | Fall asleep in goal at half-time
test: Ryan's wee sister counts eight «one mores». Best plan for next time? | Decide the number of games before starting | Promise yourself «just one more» and mean it this time | Play until somebody stops you | Hide the clock from yourself
test: Ciaran: «If I don't remember waking, my sleep was fine, so it was». True? | No, small wake-ups still break deep sleep | Yes, sleep you don't remember is perfect | Yes, buzzes only ever wake up very light sleepers | No, but phones help you sleep
test: Tyler's older cousin is asked to buy him an energy drink. Why should the cousin say no? | They're full of caffeine and not for kids | They make kids grow too fast | They're too cold to drink | They're only for goalkeepers
test: Noah: «I'll play lighter, trust» («trust» means believe me). Why is no breakfast a bad idea? | His muscles need fuel after the night | Empty stomachs make you faster | Breakfast slows your heart down before a game | Food only matters for adults
test: A cartoon gamer plays all night and then wins the real cup final. Correct the story. | A sleepless player is slower and clumsier | Gaming all night is perfect training for the real thing | Real matches are like video games | Sleep makes you forget your skills
test: Lily's mum's rule says «arvo is for gaming». When is the arvo? | The afternoon | Late at night | Early morning | Lunchtime only
test: Kai: «Bro, 2 am is fine, I'm 10, I need less sleep than my dad». Right? | No, children need more sleep than adults | Yes, kids need far less sleep | Yes, if he eats breakfast | No, they need exactly the same
exam: Identify the principal physiological effect of an intensely competitive video game played immediately prior to sleep. | The release of adrenaline, which delays the onset of sleep | The immediate induction of deep sleep | The reduction of the heart rate to its minimum | No effect of any description
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of console football to physical fitness? | It exercises principally the hands and eyes | It exercises the entire body equally | It is equivalent in every respect to training on a pitch | It improves stamina in the legs
exam: It is given that a participant commences sleep at two o'clock and rises at eight o'clock. State the duration of sleep obtained. | Six hours | Eight hours | Ten hours | Four hours
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning energy drinks and children. | They contain caffeine and are not recommended for children | They are a recommended substitute for sleep | They contain no caffeine whatsoever | They are prescribed for young athletes
